    "penalties": "Court-ordered expropriation on the Military Department’s action; voluntary divestiture available before suit",
    "summary": "Creates R.S. 29:43: the state Military Department may bring a court action to expropriate — with just compensation — immovable property within 50 miles of a military installation that was purchased, leased, possessed, or controlled by a foreign adversary or its agent or mandatary. Owners may voluntarily divest before suit is filed. The definitional move is the story: the label is “agent,” but the definition requires the person to serve as a mandatary, as provided in Louisiana Civil Code Article 2989, for a principal that is a foreign adversary, and to meet the (a)–(e) criteria. Subsection (e) is the citizenship prong — a citizen of a foreign adversary is covered unless a lawful permanent resident of the United States who does not otherwise meet (a)–(d), so an LPR meeting (a)–(d) is still an agent. Citizenship alone does not deem agent status. The enacted version contains no DHS-authorized-presence exemption — only lawful permanent residents are excluded.",
    "civilRightsImpact": "The label is “agent”; the definition is nationality. Subsection (e) brings non-LPR citizens of a foreign adversary — including Chinese students and workers on visas — into military expropriation authority where they serve as a mandatary for a foreign-adversary principal under Louisiana Civil Code Article 2989 and meet the (a)–(e) criteria. Lawful permanent residents are excluded unless they meet (a)–(d), so the individual reach is Tier 2. Covered origins: the PRC via 15 CFR 791.4 — mainland China, Hong Kong, and Macau. Not covered: U.S. citizens; lawful permanent residents; Taiwan (the ROC), which is not the PRC. Note: an earlier tracker record described a different (pre-enactment) version of this bill; this record reflects the enacted Act No. 474.",
    "keyProvisions": [
      "New R.S. 29:43: Military Department may sue to expropriate immovable property within 50 miles of a military installation held by a foreign adversary or its agent/mandatary",
      "Subsection (e): “agent”/“mandatary” means “any individual person who is a citizen of a foreign adversary, unless that person is a legal permanent resident of the United States and does not otherwise meet any of the criteria provided in Subparagraphs (a) through (d)” — and the lead-in requires serving as a mandatary under La. C.C. Art. 2989 for a foreign-adversary principal",
      "Expropriation requires just compensation; voluntary divestiture available before suit",
      "China covered via 15 C.F.R. 791.4; only LPRs excluded — no DHS-authorized-presence exemption in the enacted text"
    ],
