    "category": "property",
    "target": "“PRC national” — defined as any citizen or agent of the People’s Republic of China who is not a U.S. citizen (express citizen exclusion; no LPR exclusion). The definition’s examples of “direct, compromising ties” include PRC state-owned-enterprise employment, CCP membership, financial links to the PRC government, pledged allegiance to PRC leaders, or acting on PRC-government orders.",
    "restrictions": "Property restrictions",
    "penalties": "Civil, criminal, and administrative penalties",
    "summary": "The bill (the “New Hampshire Real Property Security Act,” new RSA 477:22-g–22-n) barred any PRC national — any citizen or agent of the PRC who is not a U.S. citizen — from acquiring or maintaining a controlling interest (ownership, lease, occupancy, or any control) in New Hampshire real property, and barred any entity from facilitating such acquisition. Current holders would have had a 90-day window for voluntary divestment; afterward, continued holding created a presumption of hostile intent, triggering state seizure and auction. Enforcement included a civil penalty of 25% of fair market value or price per violation, Class A misdemeanor liability for facilitating entities and real-estate agents, civil forfeiture without a criminal conviction (preponderance standard), a 5–10% informant bounty on net proceeds, and state facilitation of deportation proceedings. The House deemed the bill inexpedient to legislate (committee 16–0; voice vote Feb. 19, 2026).",
    "civilRightsImpact": "An explicit nationality classification with no LPR exemption: Chinese lawful permanent residents and Chinese students and workers on visas would have been barred from owning or even leasing New Hampshire real property, with seizure, a 25% penalty, and a paid-informant bounty attached — a direct restriction on housing and livelihood by nationality, hence Tier 1. Covered origins: the PRC — mainland China, Hong Kong, and Macau. Covered statuses: LPRs and visa holders who are PRC citizens. Not covered: U.S. citizens (expressly excluded from the definition); Taiwan (ROC) nationals, who are not PRC citizens. The bill failed, but its presumption-of-hostile-intent and bounty machinery is the notable civil-liberties flag in the record.",
