    "target": "A “foreign adversary company” bidding on Illinois state contracts. Section 10 defines “Company” to include “any sole proprietorship” — and a sole proprietorship has no legal identity apart from its owner — and defines “foreign adversary” as a list naming the People’s Republic of China among seven entities, plus a Governor-designation catch-all. Section 15(a) bars bids by a company domiciled in the PRC (domicile turning on registration, where affairs are primarily completed, or majority ownership), and also by companies owned or controlled by an adversary government. No citizen/LPR exemption exists anywhere in the act.",
    "restrictions": "Ineligibility for state contracts; certification and disclosure duties; 60-month debarment",
    "penalties": "$250,000 or twice the contract value per violation; contract termination; 60-month debarment",
    "summary": "Three substantively identical bills (each verified independently) create a Procurement Protection Act. The operative move: because “Company” includes “any sole proprietorship,” Section 15(a)’s ban on bids by a “foreign adversary company” — a company domiciled in the PRC or owned/controlled by an adversary government — lands on the individual owner personally. Section 15(d) penalties ($250,000 or twice the contract value, termination, 60-month debarment) and the Section 15(c)/25(a) certification duties therefore reach a self-employed person directly. Section 25(a) separately requires every bidding company to certify it has had no business operations with a foreign adversary’s military, state-owned enterprises, or political party within 60 months.",
    "civilRightsImpact": "The trigger is the owner’s own business domicile, and there is no citizen/LPR exemption: a U.S. citizen or green-card holder operating as a sole proprietor whose business is domiciled in China is barred from Illinois state contracts and personally liable for penalties — a livelihood burden imposed by status, hence Tier 1. Covered: businesses domiciled in mainland China (the text neither includes nor excludes Hong Kong or Macau). Taiwan-domiciled firms are outside the named adversary, though a Taiwan-origin owner of a PRC-domiciled business would also be barred. U.S. citizens and LPRs are covered where their business domicile triggers the bar — the text exempts no one by citizenship.",
